  "rulesOfThumb": [
    {
      "claim": "Turbo K-series on stock internals: about 400 whp is 'safe'; with pistons and rods, 600-700 whp.",
      "sourceType": "magazine/education (HP Academy blog)",
      "sources": ["https://www.hpacademy.com/blog/everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-honda-k20/"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "400-500 whp on 93 octane is very reliable on a turbo K without internal upgrades. E85 adds 30-50 whp at the same boost once tuned. A returnless system with upgraded injectors and in-tank pump tops out at about 450 hp on forced induction. A return-style conversion gives 100-150 more hp on the same injector. A 1:1 referenced FPR raises fuel pressure 1 psi per psi of boost. An aftermarket liquid intercooler on a supercharger adds 20-30 whp with tuning.",
      "sourceType": "shop (Evans Tuning)",
      "sources": ["https://www.evans-tuning.com/k-series-pretuning-checklist"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Injector power bands on E85: ID1050X 300-400 hp, ID1300X 400-550 hp, ID1700X 550-850 hp. AEM 340 L/h pump: 300-500 hp single. Walbro Hellcat: 500-800 hp single. A stock K-series makes 400-600 whp with quality parts.",
      "sourceType": "shop (Brewed Motorsports)",
      "sources": ["https://www.brewedmotorsports.com/turbo-k24-tuning-guide/"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Factory K20 ratings: K20A2 11:1, 200 hp @ 7400, 139-142 lb-ft @ 6000; K20Z1 210 hp @ 7800; K20Z3 11:1, 198 hp @ 7800, 139 lb-ft @ 6200, 8300 rpm limit; K20A (JDM) 11.5:1, 212-221 hp @ 8000.",
      "sourceType": "magazine/education (HP Academy blog)",
      "sources": ["https://www.hpacademy.com/blog/everything-you-need-to-know-about-the-honda-k20/"]
    }
  ],

